{
  $schema: "https://docs.renovatebot.com/renovate-schema.json",
  extends: [
    "config:recommended",
    "helpers:pinGitHubActionDigests",
    ":automergeBranch",
    ":dependencyDashboard",
    ":disableRateLimiting",
    ":semanticCommits",
  ],
  dependencyDashboard: true,
  dependencyDashboardTitle: "Renovate Dashboard 🤖",
  schedule: ["every weekend"],
  // Ciphertext has no versions in it, and Renovate cannot read it anyway.
  ignorePaths: ["**/*.sops.*"],
  // Look the linuxserver images up under the name Renovate recognises. `lscr.io`
  // is ghcr.io wearing a different name -- same auth realm, same digests -- but
  // Renovate has never heard of the alias, and that costs two things it gives
  // `ghcr.io` for free:
  //
  //   - GHCR returns tags *oldest-first*, so the tag that matters is on the last
  //     page. Renovate hardcodes ghcr.io in `hostsNeedingAllPages` and fetches
  //     1000 pages for it; an unrecognised host instead gets `dockerMaxPages`,
  //     default 20. radarr is 15.5k tags over 16 pages and climbing.
  //   - Renovate provisions a ghcr.io credential from its own GitHub token.
  //     Under the alias the request went out anonymous into a shared bucket,
  //     which is what was returning 429.
  //
  // Not fixable at the registry: GHCR implements only the OCI spec, which has no
  // ordering parameter -- `ordering`, `sort`, `order`, `reverse` are all ignored,
  // and `n` caps at 1000. Authenticating fixes the 429, not the order.
  //
  // `registryAliases` rewrites only the lookup. `replaceString` keeps the
  // original line, so the images stay `lscr.io` and only the tag and digest move.
  registryAliases: {
    "lscr.io": "ghcr.io",
  },
  packageRules: [
    {
      description: "Auto-merge mise tools",
      matchManagers: ["mise"],
      automerge: true,
      automergeType: "branch",
      matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch"],
      minimumReleaseAge: "3 days",
      ignoreTests: true,
    },
    {
      description: "Auto-merge GitHub Actions",
      matchManagers: ["github-actions"],
      automerge: true,
      automergeType: "branch",
      matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch", "digest"],
      minimumReleaseAge: "3 days",
      ignoreTests: true,
    },

    // --- Container images (ticket 12) ---------------------------------------
    // Every image is pinned version@digest, so Komodo's own auto_update never
    // applies: a pinned digest cannot drift. Renovate is the only thing that
    // moves a tag.
    {
      description: "Auto-merge container images",
      matchManagers: ["docker-compose"],
      automerge: true,
      automergeType: "branch",
      matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch"],
      minimumReleaseAge: "3 days",
      ignoreTests: true,
    },

    // --- linuxserver versioning ---------------------------------------------
    // Without these, Renovate offers *nothing* for any linuxserver image. Its
    // default `docker` versioning splits a tag on `-`, treats everything after
    // the first piece as a "suffix", and will only consider a candidate whose
    // suffix is identical. The `-lsNNN` build counter lives in that suffix and
    // increments on every build, so the only compatible tag is the one already
    // pinned. Simulated against the real tag lists: 1 compatible, 0 newer, for
    // all nine. That -- not the registry 429s -- is why calibre sat a major
    // version behind.
    //
    // `loose` was tried and is worse than nothing: it accepts `6.4.2-nightly`,
    // `20.04.1` (an Ubuntu tag) and bare git hashes. The `-ls(?<build>\d+)$`
    // anchor is what restricts this to the static build tags linuxserver tells
    // you to pin.
    //
    // Two traps. `revision` is only counted when `build` is also captured, so
    // the ls counter must be `build`, never `revision`. And `matchPackageNames`
    // matches packageName, which `registryAliases` above has already rewritten
    // to `ghcr.io/*` -- spelling these `lscr.io/**` silently matches nothing.
    {
      description: "linuxserver build tags — upstream version, then -lsNNN",
      matchPackageNames: [
        "ghcr.io/linuxserver/**",
        "!ghcr.io/linuxserver/qbittorrent",
        "!ghcr.io/linuxserver/lazylibrarian",
      ],
      versioning: "regex:^v?(?<major>\\d+)\\.(?<minor>\\d+)\\.(?<patch>\\d+).*-ls(?<build>\\d+)$",
    },
    {
      // Its tag has carried three shapes -- `4.4.02020…-7112-…ubuntu18.04.1-ls105`,
      // `5.1.2-r4-ls426`, now `5.2.3_v2.0.13-ls469`. The looser pattern above
      // reads the 2021 ubuntu tags as version 14 and offers a five-year rollback
      // as a major bump, so this one anchors the separator instead.
      description: "qbittorrent — tighter anchor, three tag shapes in its history",
      matchPackageNames: ["ghcr.io/linuxserver/qbittorrent"],
      versioning: "regex:^(?<major>\\d+)\\.(?<minor>\\d+)\\.(?<patch>\\d+)[-_][^-]*-ls(?<build>\\d+)$",
    },
    {
      // `ef1f6e73-ls222` — an upstream git hash and a build counter, no version
      // anywhere. Ordering on the counter alone does not work either: four
      // different images ship as `-ls328`, so it cannot name a newest. Left off
      // deliberately rather than pinned to a lie; bump it by hand.
      description: "lazylibrarian — no version in the tag to order by",
      matchPackageNames: ["ghcr.io/linuxserver/lazylibrarian"],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      // TEMPORARY — delete once the backlog is drained.
      //
      // The versioning rules above unblock updates that have never once been
      // offered, so the first run does not raise a bump, it raises a year of
      // them: radarr and prowlarr jump a minor, calibre a major, and qbittorrent
      // takes the download stack -- gluetun included -- with it. Left alone,
      // most of that automerges and redeploys unattended in one pass.
      //
      // This holds every one of them behind a tick on the dashboard, so the
      // backlog arrives as a list to work through rather than as a deploy.
      description: "linuxserver — first pass lands on the dashboard, not the box",
      matchPackageNames: ["ghcr.io/linuxserver/**"],
      dependencyDashboardApproval: true,
    },

    {
      // Still one PR — gluetun and qbittorrent must never bump apart, since
      // qbittorrent lives in gluetun's netns (ticket 06). But no longer human
      // merge: ticket 24 verified Komodo recreates both unaided, and ticket 29
      // put a probe on qbittorrent that catches 06's hazard if it ever bites.
      description: "download stack — one PR",
      matchFileNames: ["stacks/download/**"],
      groupName: "download stack",
    },
    {
      // 12 held these back because nothing was watching. Ticket 29 changed that
      // for coredns, which now has a DNS probe asserting the tailnet view, so it
      // has been released. These two have not: gatus proves plex answers, not
      // that a client can play from it, and a broken Caddy costs the same to
      // repair however fast you hear about it.
      description: "plex, caddy — human merge",
      matchFileNames: [
        "stacks/plex/**",
        "stacks/caddy/**",
      ],
      automerge: false,
    },
    // ntfy and gatus were human-merge because nothing on tower watched the
    // watcher. Retired as ticket 29 said it would be: home-ops now probes both
    // from off-box, so a bad bump here is seen by something that does not share
    // their failure.

    // --- Bootstrap (ticket 12) ----------------------------------------------
    // Komodo cannot deploy itself, so bootstrap has no komodo.toml and nothing
    // reconciles it. Renovate raises the bump; a human applies it on the box.
    {
      description: "Komodo Core + Periphery bump as a pair",
      matchFileNames: ["bootstrap/**"],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "ghcr.io/moghtech/komodo-core",
        "ghcr.io/moghtech/komodo-periphery",
      ],
      groupName: "komodo",
      automerge: false,
      prBodyNotes: [
        "⚠️ **Bootstrap is not reconciled by Komodo.** Merging this changes nothing on the box.",
        "```sh",
        "just bootstrap            # what differs, and what would run",
        "just bootstrap --apply    # copy it over and recreate the containers",
        "```",
        "Core and Periphery must move together — upstream requires Periphery match Core's version before builds and clones work again.",
      ],
    },
    {
      description: "FerretDB + postgres-documentdb bump as a pair",
      matchFileNames: ["bootstrap/**"],
      matchPackageNames: [
        "ghcr.io/ferretdb/ferretdb",
        "ghcr.io/ferretdb/postgres-documentdb",
      ],
      groupName: "ferretdb",
      automerge: false,
      prBodyNotes: [
        "⚠️ **Bootstrap is not reconciled by Komodo.** Merging this changes nothing on the box.",
        "```sh",
        "just bootstrap            # what differs, and what would run",
        "just bootstrap --apply    # copy it over and recreate the containers",
        "```",
        "The `postgres-documentdb` tag names the FerretDB release it was built against, so the two must move together. Upstream warns these updates can be breaking — back up Postgres first.",
      ],
    },
  ],
}
